-
**Remove.**
Click
a
connection,
or
focus
it
and
press
Enter
or
Space,
to
select
it.
Then
press
Delete
or
Backspace
with
focus
on
the
canvas,
or
use
**Remove
connection**
in
the
selected
connection's
toolbar
beside
**Reset
route**:
it
is
32
px
and
44
px
under
a
coarse
pointer,
is
disabled
with
the
store's
reason
when
the
store
would
refuse
the
removal,
and
moves
focus
to
**Undo**
afterwards.
It
acts
on
a
click
the
browser
counts
as
0
or
1
(Enter,
Space
and
a
script's
`click()`
report
0)
and
not
on
a
later
click
of
a
double
or
triple
click,
which
the
browser
counts
above
1
(#207).
Clicking
a
connection's
line
selects
it
and
draws
its
toolbar
under
the
pointer,
so
the
second
click
of
a
double
click
can
land
on
**Remove
connection**;
it
then
removes
nothing
and
moves
focus
back
to
the
connection,
where
Delete
or
Backspace
still
removes
it.
Its
click
ends
on
the
button
first,
so
Control
or
Meta
cannot
select
the
connection
again
and
toggle
it
off.
The
rule
follows
the
browser's
count,
not
timing
or
position:
a
separate
click
that
the
browser
counts
as
1
removes
the
connection,
and
one
Undo
restores
it.
How
a
browser
counts
touch
or
pen
clicks,
and
a
run
of
more
than
three,
is
not
established
here.
One
connection
is
removed
at
a
time;
a
selected
node
is
never
removed
by
an
edge's
removal.
The
store's
`removeEdge`
removes
exactly
one
stored
record
and
refuses,
without
changing
the
draft,
its
history,
revision,
save
state
or
route
ownership,
a
protected
voice
step
("Voice
steps
stay.")
and
an
id
more
than
one
stored
edge
holds
("More
than
one
connection
uses
the
ID
"e1";
this
action
cannot
choose
just
one."),
as
an
imported
or
API-written
draft
can
(`DUPLICATE_EDGE_ID`).
The
shared
id
is
checked
first,
so
a
protected
holder
is
refused
the
same
way
in
either
stored
order;
an
absent
id
changes
nothing
and
says
nothing.
The
button's
disabled
reason
is
the
same
string
(`edgeRemovalRefusal`
in
`editor/store.ts`),
and
Delete
or
Backspace
shows
it
in
the
"Connection
refused:
…"
notice.
A
selection
belongs
to
the
connection
selected:
its
load
of
the
draft
(the
store's
`generation`),
its
id
with
the
stored
ends
of
every
edge
holding
it,
and
its
path
on
the
canvas.
A
load
into
the
mounted
editor
(**Reload
server
draft**,
a
device
restore,
a
server
reconcile),
even
of
the
same
id
and
ends,
an
id
now
held
with
other
ends
(a
renamed
end,
a
Decision
route
ID
renamed)
and
a
port
that
is
gone
(a
Script
exit-code
label
removed,
a
Decision
route
ID
left
blank)
forget
it
while
rendering,
and
Undo
does
not
bring
it
back.
Moves,
route
edits,
label
edits,
config
edits
that
keep
the
ports,
issue
refreshes
and
their
Undo
keep
it.
**Remove
connection**,
**Reset
route**,
a
nudge,
a
released
route
drag
and
Delete
or
Backspace
check
the
selection
again
when
they
act,
since
rendering
can
lag
the
store.
One
left
over
from
before
such
a
change,
or
a
route
drag
held
across
a
load
or
a
new
selection
(even
of
the
same
id),
changes
nothing,
says
"That
connection
changed
since
it
was
selected;
nothing
changed."
in
the
canvas's
polite
live
region,
drops
the
selection
and
moves
focus
to
**Undo**.
With
a
connection
selected,
Delete
and
Backspace
belong
to
it:
they
remove
it,
or
the
store
refuses
and
the
selection
stays,
or
it
changed
and
nothing
happens;
they
never
go
on
to
a
selected
node
in
the
same
press.
Once
the
canvas
has
forgotten
a
selection,
they
remove
the
selected
node
as
usual
(after
a
Control-click
or
a
Loop
issues
entry
opened
a
card
whose
connection
then
lost
its
port,
say).
After
a
mouse
pan
or
a
mouse
drag
of
a
card
the
toolbar
briefly
refuses:
d3-zoom
ends
a
pan
that
moved,
and
d3-drag
(xyflow's
XYDrag)
a
card
drag
past
the
click
distance,
by
adding
a
window-capture
`click.drag`
listener
(d3-drag's
`yesdrag`)
that
cancels
the
next
click
anywhere
on
the
page,
and
removes
it
in
a
zero-delay
task,
so
a
Remove
connection
click
in
between
was
swallowed
while
the
button
looked
available
(a
card
dragged
with
Control
held
keeps
the
connection
selected).
A
per-canvas
gate
(`editor/viewport-gesture.ts`)
is
`panning`
from
the
canvas's
first
`onMove`
with
a
`mousemove`
source
event,
or
from
`onNodeDragStart`
with
one,
`settling`
from
the
next
mouse
release,
and
ready
two
tasks
later.
XYDrag
starts
a
card
drag
on
the
`mousemove`
past
its
node
drag
threshold,
which
the
canvas
sets
to
the
click
distance
it
gives
d3-drag,
both
measured
in
client
pixels
from
the
press,
so
the
gate
is
armed
exactly
for
the
drags
whose
release
adds
the
blocker.
Its
single
passive
window-capture
`mouseup`
listener
is
added
when
the
canvas
mounts,
so
it
sees
each
release
before
d3-zoom's
or
d3-drag's
per-press
listener
stops
it;
it
queues
a
zero-delay
task
that
queues
a
second,
and
only
the
second,
which
runs
after
d3's
cleanup,
makes
it
ready,
and
only
if
no
mouse
move
came
since
and
the
canvas
is
still
mounted.
Meanwhile
**Remove
connection**
and
**Reset
route**
are
`aria-disabled`
(dimmed,
keeping
focus,
their
hit
target
and
`nopan`)
with
`data-gesture`
set
to
the
state,
and
their
handlers
refuse
on
that
same
rendered
state:
a
click,
Enter
or
Space
then
does
nothing
and
says
nothing,
and
the
first
click
once
they
show
as
available
acts
once.
The
gesture's
own
click
is
still
cancelled
and
the
selection
kept.
Wheel,
touch
(a
touch
drag
of
a
card
too),
keyboard,
Fit
View
and
other
programmatic
moves
add
no
blocker
and
never
touch
the
gate;
they
also
cannot
strand
it,
since
it
does
not
wait
for
xyflow's
`onMoveEnd`,
which
a
later
wheel
or
programmatic
end
replaces.
A
release
that
never
reaches
the
page
leaves
the
gate
unavailable
until
the
next
mouse
release
there,
which
also
ends
d3's
gesture.
Nothing
is
replayed,
prevented
or
patched;
the
store's
refusal
keeps
its
own
disabled
reason.
The
Space
cards
offer's
buttons
(#208,
"Space
cards"
above)
follow
this
same
gate.
Clicks
on
xyflow's
zoom
controls
meet
the
same
d3
blocker
and
are
not
gated.
The
screen-reader
description
of
a
focused
connection
says
"Press
Enter
or
Space
to
select
the
connection,
then
Delete
or
Backspace
to
remove
it;
its
toolbar
also
has
Remove
connection.
To
connect
its
output
elsewhere,
remove
the
connection
and
draw
from
the
output
again."
